%[text] # MakeFlyEasy Believer Powered-Flight Performance Model
%[text] This live script develops a first-principles aerodynamic performance model for the MakeFlyEasy Believer airframe. 
%[text] The aerodynamic coefficients used for the nominal powered-flight model are the **RPM-derived power-on coefficients** reported by Weishäupl, McLay and Sóbester in their article “Experimental evaluation of the drag curves of small fixed wing UAVs", Published in *The Aeronautical Journal*, vol. 128 (2024). Those coefficients were identified from actual powered flight using measured propeller RPM, airspeed, and a propeller performance map to estimate thrust.
%[text] The paper models the drag polar as
%[text] $C\_D=C\_{D0}+K C\_L^2$
%[text] and the linear lift curve as
%[text] $C\_L=C\_{L0}+C\_{L\_\\alpha}\\alpha$
%[text] where $C\_{L\_\\alpha}$ is reported in $\\mathrm{deg}^{-1}$.
%[text] ## 1. Modelling assumptions
%[text] The first version of the model is a **steady, point-mass performance model**. It is therefore intended to answer questions such as required lift, drag, power, glide performance, climb capability, and coordinated-turn geometry. It is not yet a six-degree-of-freedom flight-dynamics model.
%[text] 
%[text] The principal assumptions are:
%[text] - Low-Mach-number incompressible flow.
%[text] - Constant air density unless explicitly changed.
%[text] - Steady level flight for the derivation of the baseline $C\_L(V)$ and drag curve.
%[text] - The aerodynamic coefficients are treated as constant fits over the operating range.
%[text] - The nominal powered-flight polar is the RPM-derived polar from the paper.
%[text] - Thrust is treated separately from the aerodynamic drag model. Final climb and maximum-speed predictions should use thrust as a function of airspeed, not a constant static-thrust value. \
%[text] 
%[text] ## 2. Environment and aircraft inputs
%[text] Gravitational acceleration converts aircraft mass into weight:
%[text] $W=mg$
%[text] Weight has units of newtons, whereas mass has units of kilograms.
g   = 9.80665;          % gravitational acceleration [m/s^2]
rho = 1.225;            % ISA sea-level air density [kg/m^3]

%[text] The paper reports a wing span of $b=1.96\\,\\mathrm{m}$, reference wing area $S=0.488\\,\\mathrm{m^2}$, mean aerodynamic chord $\\bar c=0.275\\,\\mathrm{m}$, a nominal 1-g stall speed of $11\\,\\mathrm{m/s}$, recommended cruise speed of $20\\,\\mathrm{m/s}$, and maximum dive / never-exceed speed of $37\\,\\mathrm{m/s}$.
b     = 1.96;           % wingspan [m]
S     = 0.488;          % reference wing area [m^2]
c_bar = 0.275;          % mean aerodynamic chord [m]

V_stall  = 11.0;        % reference 1-g stall airspeed [m/s]
V_cruise = 20.0;        % recommended cruise airspeed [m/s]
V_NE     = 37.0;        % never-exceed / maximum dive airspeed [m/s]

%[text] **Aircraft mass is a user input.** The paper's $5.5\\,\\mathrm{kg}$ value is the maximum allowable take-off mass, not necessarily the mass used in every flight. Replace the value below with the measured ready-to-fly mass of the aircraft being modelled.
mass = 4.00;            % USER INPUT: actual aircraft mass [kg]
W = mass * g;           % aircraft weight [N]

%[text] The geometric aspect ratio is defined as
%[text] $AR=\\frac{b^2}{S}$
%[text] It measures how long and slender the wing is. This is a purely geometric quantity.
AR = b^2 / S;

%[text] ## 3. Powered-flight aerodynamic coefficients
%[text] For the normal **props-spinning** flight model, use the RPM-derived power-on coefficients:
%[text:table]
%[text] | Coefficient | Value | Meaning |
%[text] | --- | --- | --- |
%[text] | $C\_{D0}$ | 0\.0317 | zero-lift / parasite-drag coefficient |
%[text] | $K$ | 0\.0998 | induced-drag factor |
%[text] | $C\_{L0}$ | 0\.3719 | lift coefficient at zero angle of attack |
%[text] | $C\_{L\_\\alpha}$ | 0\.0583 deg$^{-1}$ | slope of the linear lift curve |
%[text:table]
CD0     = 0.0317;
K       = 0.0998;
CL0     = 0.3719;
CLalpha = 0.0583;       % [1/deg]

%[text] The induced-drag factor is commonly related to aspect ratio and Oswald span efficiency by
%[text] $K=\\frac{1}{\\pi AR e}$
%[text] Rearranging gives
%[text] $e=\\frac{1}{\\pi ARK}$
%[text] We can therefore calculate the Oswald efficiency implied by the measured drag polar. This does **not** mean the authors directly measured $e$; it is simply the value consistent with the fitted $K$ and the aircraft geometry.
e_implied = 1 / (pi * AR * K);

%[text] ## 4. Airspeed range and dynamic pressure
%[text] The aerodynamic force equations are conventionally written using **dynamic pressure**:
%[text] $q=\\frac{1}{2}\\rho V^2$
%[text] This comes from the kinetic-energy density of the moving air. Dynamic pressure has units of pascals, or equivalently $\\mathrm{N/m^2}$.
V = linspace(V_stall, V_NE, 1000);   % airspeed sweep [m/s]
q = 0.5 * rho .* V.^2;               % dynamic pressure [Pa]

%[text] ## 5. Lift equation and required lift coefficient
%[text] The definition of lift coefficient is obtained by nondimensionalising lift:
%[text] $L=qSC\_L$
%[text] Substituting the definition of dynamic pressure gives
%[text] $L=\\frac{1}{2}\\rho V^2SC\_L$
%[text] 
%[text] For steady, straight, level flight, the vertical acceleration is zero, so the vertical forces balance:
%[text] $L=W$
%[text] Therefore,
%[text] $W=\\frac{1}{2}\\rho V^2SC\_L$
%[text] Solving for the lift coefficient required to support the aircraft gives
%[text] $C\_L(V)=\\frac{2W}{\\rho V^2S}$
%[text] This equation is important: as airspeed decreases, the aircraft must operate at a larger lift coefficient to support the same weight.
CL_req = W ./ (q .* S);

%[text] ## 6. Angle of attack required for level flight
%[text] The paper fits the lift curve using the linear relation
%[text] $C\_L=C\_{L0}+C\_{L\_\\alpha}\\alpha$
%[text] Solving for angle of attack,
%[text] $C\_L-C\_{L0}=C\_{L\_\\alpha}\\alpha$
%[text] and hence
%[text] $\\alpha=\\frac{C\_L-C\_{L0}}{C\_{L\_\\alpha}}$
%[text] Because the paper reports $C\_{L\_\\alpha}$ in $\\mathrm{deg}^{-1}$, the resulting $\\alpha$ is in degrees.
alpha_req_deg = (CL_req - CL0) ./ CLalpha;

%[text] ## 7. Drag polar
%[text] The paper represents the aircraft drag coefficient using the parabolic drag polar
%[text] $C\_D=C\_{D0}+KC\_L^2$
%[text] The two terms represent different physical mechanisms:
%[text] - $C\_{D0}$ approximates parasite drag: fuselage drag, skin friction, interference drag, and other drag that does not depend directly on lift.
%[text] - $KC\_L^2$ approximates induced drag caused by generating lift. \
CD = CD0 + K .* CL_req.^2;

%[text] ## 8. From drag coefficient to drag force
%[text] Drag coefficient is defined analogously to lift coefficient:
%[text] $D=qSC\_D$
%[text] Substituting the dynamic pressure and the drag polar,
%[text] $D=\\frac{1}{2}\\rho V^2S\\left(C\_{D0}+KC\_L^2\\right)$
%[text] Splitting the expression into two terms gives
%[text] $D=\\underbrace{\\frac{1}{2}\\rho V^2SC\_{D0}}\_{D\_0}+\\underbrace{\\frac{1}{2}\\rho V^2SKC\_L^2}\_{D\_i}$
%[text] where $D\_0$ is parasite drag and $D\_i$ is induced drag.
D_parasite = q .* S .* CD0;
D_induced  = q .* S .* K .* CL_req.^2;
D_total    = D_parasite + D_induced;

%[text] ### 8.1 Deriving drag directly as a function of airspeed
%[text] In level flight,
%[text] $C\_L=\\frac{2W}{\\rho V^2S}$
%[text] Substitute this into the induced-drag term:
%[text] $D\_i=\\frac{1}{2}\\rho V^2SK\\left(\\frac{2W}{\\rho V^2S}\\right)^2$
%[text] Squaring the bracket gives
%[text] $D\_i=\\frac{1}{2}\\rho V^2SK\\frac{4W^2}{\\rho^2V^4S^2}$
%[text] Cancelling common factors,
%[text] $D\_i=\\frac{2KW^2}{\\rho V^2S}$
%[text] Therefore the complete level-flight drag equation can be written
%[text] $D(V)=\\frac{1}{2}\\rho SC\_{D0}V^2+\\frac{2KW^2}{\\rho S}\\frac{1}{V^2}$
%[text] or, more compactly,
%[text] $D(V)=AV^2+\\frac{B}{V^2}$
%[text] with
%[text] $A=\\frac{1}{2}\\rho SC\_{D0},\\qquad B=\\frac{2KW^2}{\\rho S}$
%[text] This explains the familiar U-shaped drag curve: induced drag dominates at low speed and parasite drag dominates at high speed.
A_drag = 0.5 * rho * S * CD0;
B_drag = 2 * K * W^2 / (rho * S);
D_direct = A_drag .* V.^2 + B_drag ./ V.^2;

%[text] The two independently coded forms should agree to numerical precision. The quantity below is retained as a useful implementation check.
drag_equation_error = max(abs(D_total - D_direct));

%[text] ## 9. Lift-to-drag ratio
%[text] Since
%[text] $L=qSC\_L,\\qquad D=qSC\_D,$
%[text] their ratio is
%[text] $\\frac{L}{D}=\\frac{qSC\_L}{qSC\_D}$
%[text] and the common factors cancel:
%[text] $\\frac{L}{D}=\\frac{C\_L}{C\_D}$
%[text] For an unpowered aircraft, $L/D$ is also closely related to glide distance: in still air and for shallow glide angles, a glide ratio of 8 corresponds to roughly 8 units of horizontal distance per unit of altitude lost.
LD = CL_req ./ CD;

%[text] ## 10. Maximum lift-to-drag ratio
%[text] Using the parabolic polar,
%[text] $\\frac{L}{D}=\\frac{C\_L}{C\_{D0}+KC\_L^2}$
%[text] To find the $C\_L$ that maximises $L/D$, differentiate with respect to $C\_L$ and set the derivative equal to zero:
%[text] $\\frac{d}{dC\_L}\\left(\\frac{C\_L}{C\_{D0}+KC\_L^2}\\right)=0$
%[text] Applying the quotient rule gives a numerator proportional to
%[text] $C\_{D0}+KC\_L^2-2KC\_L^2=C\_{D0}-KC\_L^2$
%[text] Therefore,
%[text] $C\_{D0}-KC\_L^2=0$
%[text] so
%[text] $C\_{L,(L/D)\_{\\max}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{C\_{D0}}{K}}$
%[text] At this condition, parasite and induced drag coefficients are equal:
%[text] $C\_{D0}=KC\_L^2$
%[text] Substitution into $L/D$ gives
%[text] $\\left(\\frac{L}{D}\\right)\_{\\max}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{C\_{D0}K}}$
CL_LDmax = sqrt(CD0 / K);
LDmax_analytic = 1 / (2 * sqrt(CD0 * K));

%[text] To obtain the corresponding airspeed, start again from the level-flight lift equation:
%[text] $C\_L=\\frac{2W}{\\rho V^2S}$
%[text] Rearranging for $V$,
%[text] $V^2=\\frac{2W}{\\rho SC\_L}$
%[text] and therefore
%[text] $V=\\sqrt{\\frac{2W}{\\rho SC\_L}}$
%[text] Evaluating this at $C\_{L,(L/D)\_{\\max}}$ gives the best-$L/D$ airspeed.
V_LDmax_analytic = sqrt((2 * W) / (rho * S * CL_LDmax));

[LDmax_numeric, idxLD] = max(LD);
V_LDmax_numeric = V(idxLD);

%[text] ## 11. Aerodynamic power required
%[text] Mechanical power is force multiplied by velocity in the direction of that force:
%[text] $P=\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathbf{V}$
%[text] In steady level flight, thrust must balance drag, so the useful propulsive power required by the airframe is
%[text] $P\_R=DV$
%[text] This is **aerodynamic power**, not battery electrical power. Battery power will be larger because the motor, ESC, and propeller are not 100% efficient.
P_required = D_total .* V;

[Pmin, idxP] = min(P_required);
V_minPower = V(idxP);

%[text] ## 12. Power-off glide and sink rate
%[text] During a steady unpowered glide, the aircraft loses gravitational potential energy. Potential energy is
%[text] $E\_p=mgh=Wh$
%[text] Differentiating with respect to time gives the rate of loss of potential energy:
%[text] $-\\frac{dE\_p}{dt}=W\\left(-\\frac{dh}{dt}\\right)$
%[text] The aerodynamic drag dissipates energy at the rate
%[text] $P\_D=DV$
%[text] For a shallow steady glide, equating these gives approximately
%[text] $W V\_{\\mathrm{sink}}\\approx DV$
%[text] and hence
%[text] $V\_{\\mathrm{sink}}\\approx\\frac{DV}{W}=\\frac{P\_R}{W}$
%[text] This is a point-mass approximation. A more exact glide model can later retain the finite glide angle explicitly.
sink_rate = P_required ./ W;
sink_min = min(sink_rate);

%[text] ## 13. Stall-speed consistency check
%[text] At the 1-g stall boundary, the aircraft is assumed to have reached its maximum usable lift coefficient $C\_{L,\\max}$. Setting lift equal to weight gives
%[text] $W=\\frac{1}{2}\\rho V\_S^2S C\_{L,\\max}$
%[text] so
%[text] $C\_{L,\\max}=\\frac{2W}{\\rho V\_S^2S}$
%[text] However, this is only valid if the chosen $V\_S$, mass, density, and configuration describe the **same aircraft condition**. Because the paper quotes a reference stall speed while the actual ready-to-fly mass may differ, the value below should currently be treated as a consistency check rather than an independently measured $C\_{L,\\max}$.
CL_required_at_stall = 2 * W / (rho * V_stall^2 * S);
alpha_required_at_stall = (CL_required_at_stall - CL0) / CLalpha;

%[text] ## 14. Coordinated level turns
%[text] In a banked coordinated turn, the lift vector is tilted by bank angle $\\phi$. Its vertical component must still balance weight:
%[text] $L\\cos\\phi=W$
%[text] Therefore
%[text] $L=\\frac{W}{\\cos\\phi}$
%[text] The load factor is defined as
%[text] $n=\\frac{L}{W}$
%[text] giving
%[text] $n=\\frac{1}{\\cos\\phi}$
%[text] 
%[text] Because stall occurs at a fixed maximum lift coefficient, and lift varies with $V^2$, the stall speed increases with the square root of load factor:
%[text] $V\_{S,\\phi}=V\_S\\sqrt{n}$
%[text] 
%[text] The horizontal component of lift supplies the centripetal force:
%[text] $L\\sin\\phi=\\frac{mV^2}{R}$
%[text] Divide this equation by the vertical-force equation $L\\cos\\phi=mg$:
%[text] $\\tan\\phi=\\frac{V^2}{gR}$
%[text] Rearranging,
%[text] $R=\\frac{V^2}{g\\tan\\phi}$
%[text] The corresponding turn rate is
%[text] $\\dot\\psi=\\frac{V}{R}=\\frac{g\\tan\\phi}{V}$
bank_deg = [20 30 45 60];
turn = struct();

for i = 1:numel(bank_deg)
    phi = deg2rad(bank_deg(i));
    n = 1 / cos(phi);

    Vstall_turn = V_stall * sqrt(n);
    R = V.^2 ./ (g * tan(phi));
    turnRate_deg_s = rad2deg(g * tan(phi) ./ V);

    invalid = V < Vstall_turn;
    R(invalid) = NaN;
    turnRate_deg_s(invalid) = NaN;

    turn(i).bank_deg = bank_deg(i);
    turn(i).n = n;
    turn(i).Vstall = Vstall_turn;
    turn(i).R = R;
    turn(i).turnRate_deg_s = turnRate_deg_s;
end

%[text] ## 15. Propulsion model: deliberately separated from aerodynamics
%[text] The drag polar tells us the **thrust required** for steady level flight because
%[text] $T\_{\\mathrm{required}}=D$
%[text] but it does not tell us the thrust that the propulsion system can actually produce.
%[text] 
%[text] For maximum speed and climb calculations we require a model of
%[text] $T\_{\\mathrm{available}}(V)$
%[text] for the actual two-motor, 12x6-propeller propulsion system.
%[text] 
%[text] A static thrust measurement gives $T(0)$, but it should not be treated as constant across the flight envelope. The preferred final implementation is a table or propeller model containing total thrust from **both motors** as a function of airspeed.
prop.mode = "none";

%[text] Use `prop.mode = "table"` when suitable thrust-versus-airspeed data are available.
%[text] The arrays should contain **total aircraft thrust** from both propellers.
prop.V_mps = [];        % e.g. [0 5 10 15 20 25 30 35]
prop.T_N   = [];        % corresponding total thrust [N]

%[text] A constant-thrust mode is retained only as a rough engineering sanity check.
prop.constantThrust_N = NaN;

T_available = nan(size(V));

switch prop.mode
    case "none"
        % Aerodynamic model only.

    case "constant"
        if ~isfinite(prop.constantThrust_N)
            error("Set prop.constantThrust_N before using constant-thrust mode.");
        end
        T_available = prop.constantThrust_N * ones(size(V));

    case "table"
        if numel(prop.V_mps) < 2 || numel(prop.T_N) ~= numel(prop.V_mps)
            error("Provide matching prop.V_mps and prop.T_N vectors.");
        end
        T_available = interp1(prop.V_mps, prop.T_N, V, "pchip", NaN);

    otherwise
        error("Unknown propulsion mode.");
end

propulsionEnabled = any(isfinite(T_available));

%[text] ## 16. Excess thrust, excess power, and climb rate
%[text] When the propulsion model is enabled, the force available to accelerate or climb is the excess thrust:
%[text] $T\_{\\mathrm{excess}}=T\_{\\mathrm{available}}-D$
%[text] Multiplying by airspeed gives excess power:
%[text] $P\_{\\mathrm{excess}}=(T\_{\\mathrm{available}}-D)V$
%[text] 
%[text] In a steady climb, the excess propulsive power is used to increase gravitational potential energy:
%[text] $P\_{\\mathrm{excess}}=W\\frac{dh}{dt}$
%[text] Therefore,
%[text] $\\mathrm{ROC}=\\frac{dh}{dt}=\\frac{(T\_{\\mathrm{available}}-D)V}{W}$
%[text] This is the standard point-mass excess-power relationship.
if propulsionEnabled
    excess_thrust = T_available - D_total;
    excess_power  = excess_thrust .* V;
    ROC = excess_power ./ W;

    feasibleLevel = isfinite(T_available) & (T_available >= D_total);

    if any(feasibleLevel)
        V_max_level = max(V(feasibleLevel));
    else
        V_max_level = NaN;
    end

    [ROC_max, idxROC] = max(ROC);
    V_bestROC = V(idxROC);
else
    excess_thrust = nan(size(V));
    excess_power = nan(size(V));
    ROC = nan(size(V));
    V_max_level = NaN;
    ROC_max = NaN;
    V_bestROC = NaN;
end

%[text] ## 17. Numerical summary
%[text] The following output provides useful checks before examining the figures. In particular, `drag_equation_error` should be close to machine precision because the coefficient-based and direct $D(V)$ equations are mathematically equivalent.
fprintf(("\n" + ... %[output:group:7154f3e1] %[output:4314c6b1]
    "============================================================\n" + ... %[output:4314c6b1]
    " BELIEVER POWERED-FLIGHT PERFORMANCE MODEL\n" + ... %[output:4314c6b1]
    "============================================================\n" + ... %[output:4314c6b1]
    "Mass:                          %.3f kg\n" + ... %[output:4314c6b1]
    "Weight:                        %.2f N\n" + ... %[output:4314c6b1]
    "Wing area:                     %.3f m^2\n" + ... %[output:4314c6b1]
    "Aspect ratio:                  %.3f\n" + ... %[output:4314c6b1]
    "CD0:                           %.4f\n" + ... %[output:4314c6b1]
    "K:                             %.4f\n" + ... %[output:4314c6b1]
    "CL0:                           %.4f\n" + ... %[output:4314c6b1]
    "CLalpha:                       %.4f 1/deg\n" + ... %[output:4314c6b1]
    "Implied Oswald efficiency:     %.3f\n" + ... %[output:4314c6b1]
    "Max |D-Ddirect|:               %.3e N\n" + ... %[output:4314c6b1]
    "\n" + ... %[output:4314c6b1]
    "Analytic max L/D:              %.3f\n" + ... %[output:4314c6b1]
    "Analytic V at max L/D:         %.2f m/s\n" + ... %[output:4314c6b1]
    "Numeric max L/D in envelope:   %.3f\n" + ... %[output:4314c6b1]
    "Numeric V at max L/D:          %.2f m/s\n" + ... %[output:4314c6b1]
    "Minimum aero-power speed:      %.2f m/s\n" + ... %[output:4314c6b1]
    "Minimum approximate sink:      %.2f m/s\n" + ... %[output:4314c6b1]
    "CL required at %.1f m/s:        %.3f\n" + ... %[output:4314c6b1]
    "Equivalent alpha at %.1f m/s:   %.2f deg\n"), ... %[output:4314c6b1]
    mass, W, S, AR, CD0, K, CL0, CLalpha, e_implied, drag_equation_error, ... %[output:4314c6b1]
    LDmax_analytic, V_LDmax_analytic, LDmax_numeric, V_LDmax_numeric, ... %[output:4314c6b1]
    V_minPower, sink_min, V_stall, CL_required_at_stall, V_stall, alpha_required_at_stall); %[output:group:7154f3e1] %[output:4314c6b1]

if propulsionEnabled %[output:group:6323b6ca]
    fprintf(("\n" + ...
        "Max level-flight speed:        %.2f m/s\n" + ...
        "Maximum rate of climb:         %.2f m/s\n" + ...
        "Best ROC speed:                %.2f m/s\n"), ...
        V_max_level, ROC_max, V_bestROC);
else
    fprintf("\nPropulsion model disabled: add T_available(V) for climb/max-speed results.\n"); %[output:94188e0d]
end %[output:group:6323b6ca]
fprintf("============================================================\n\n"); %[output:48a1a06d]

%[text] ## 18. Plot formatting
%[text] Each result is intentionally placed in a separate figure so that figures can be inspected, exported, and cited independently. Plot titles, labels, legends, and annotations use the LaTeX interpreter.
set(groot, ...
    "defaultTextInterpreter", "latex", ...
    "defaultAxesTickLabelInterpreter", "latex", ...
    "defaultLegendInterpreter", "latex");

%[text] ### Drag versus airspeed
figure("Name", "Drag vs Airspeed"); %[output:31d7eb6e]
plot(V, D_total, "LineWidth", 1.8); %[output:31d7eb6e]
hold on; %[output:31d7eb6e]
plot(V, D_parasite, "--", "LineWidth", 1.4); %[output:31d7eb6e]
plot(V, D_induced, "--", "LineWidth", 1.4); %[output:31d7eb6e]
xline(V_stall, ":", "$V_S$", "Interpreter", "latex", "LabelVerticalAlignment", "bottom"); %[output:31d7eb6e]
xline(V_cruise, ":", "$V_{\mathrm{cruise}}$", "Interpreter", "latex", "LabelVerticalAlignment", "bottom"); %[output:31d7eb6e]
xline(V_NE, ":", "$V_{NE}$", "Interpreter", "latex", "LabelVerticalAlignment", "bottom"); %[output:31d7eb6e]
grid on; %[output:31d7eb6e]
xlabel("True airspeed, $V$ [m/s]"); %[output:31d7eb6e]
ylabel("Drag, $D$ [N]"); %[output:31d7eb6e]
title("Aircraft Drag vs Airspeed"); %[output:31d7eb6e]
legend("$D$", "$D_0$", "$D_i$", "Location", "best"); %[output:31d7eb6e]

%[text] ### Lift-to-drag ratio
figure("Name", "Lift-to-Drag Ratio"); %[output:62c39589]
plot(V, LD, "LineWidth", 1.8); %[output:62c39589]
hold on; %[output:62c39589]
xline(V_LDmax_analytic, "--", "$V_{(L/D)_{\max}}$", "Interpreter", "latex", "LabelVerticalAlignment", "bottom"); %[output:62c39589]
xline(V_cruise, ":", "$V_{\mathrm{cruise}}$", "Interpreter", "latex", "LabelVerticalAlignment", "bottom"); %[output:62c39589]
grid on; %[output:62c39589]
xlabel("True airspeed, $V$ [m/s]"); %[output:62c39589]
ylabel("$L/D$"); %[output:62c39589]
title("Lift-to-Drag Ratio vs Airspeed"); %[output:62c39589]

%[text] ### Aerodynamic power required
figure("Name", "Power Required"); %[output:83491f40]
plot(V, P_required, "LineWidth", 1.8); %[output:83491f40]
hold on; %[output:83491f40]
xline(V_minPower, "--", "$V_{P_{\min}}$", "Interpreter", "latex", "LabelVerticalAlignment", "bottom"); %[output:83491f40]
xline(V_cruise, ":", "$V_{\mathrm{cruise}}$", "Interpreter", "latex", "LabelVerticalAlignment", "bottom"); %[output:83491f40]
grid on; %[output:83491f40]
xlabel("True airspeed, $V$ [m/s]"); %[output:83491f40]
ylabel("Aerodynamic power required, $P_R$ [W]"); %[output:83491f40]
title("Aerodynamic Power Required vs Airspeed"); %[output:83491f40]

%[text] ### Approximate power-off sink rate
figure("Name", "Glide Sink Rate"); %[output:67135457]
plot(V, sink_rate, "LineWidth", 1.8); %[output:67135457]
hold on; %[output:67135457]
xline(V_minPower, "--", "$V_{\mathrm{min\,sink}}$", "Interpreter", "latex", "LabelVerticalAlignment", "bottom"); %[output:67135457]
grid on; %[output:67135457]
xlabel("True airspeed, $V$ [m/s]"); %[output:67135457]
ylabel("Approximate sink rate [m/s]"); %[output:67135457]
title("Approximate Power-Off Sink Rate"); %[output:67135457]

%[text] ### Required lift coefficient
figure("Name", "Required Lift Coefficient"); %[output:7f5e1aef]
plot(V, CL_req, "LineWidth", 1.8); %[output:7f5e1aef]
hold on; %[output:7f5e1aef]
xline(V_stall, ":", "$V_S$", "Interpreter", "latex", "LabelVerticalAlignment", "bottom"); %[output:7f5e1aef]
xline(V_cruise, ":", "$V_{\mathrm{cruise}}$", "Interpreter", "latex", "LabelVerticalAlignment", "bottom"); %[output:7f5e1aef]
grid on; %[output:7f5e1aef]
xlabel("True airspeed, $V$ [m/s]"); %[output:7f5e1aef]
ylabel("Required lift coefficient, $C_L$"); %[output:7f5e1aef]
title("Required $C_L$ for Steady Level Flight"); %[output:7f5e1aef]

%[text] ### Required angle of attack
figure("Name", "Required Angle of Attack"); %[output:0ffdc851]
plot(V, alpha_req_deg, "LineWidth", 1.8); %[output:0ffdc851]
hold on; %[output:0ffdc851]
xline(V_stall, ":", "$V_S$", "Interpreter", "latex", "LabelVerticalAlignment", "bottom"); %[output:0ffdc851]
xline(V_cruise, ":", "$V_{\mathrm{cruise}}$", "Interpreter", "latex", "LabelVerticalAlignment", "bottom"); %[output:0ffdc851]
yline(0, ":"); %[output:0ffdc851]
grid on; %[output:0ffdc851]
xlabel("True airspeed, $V$ [m/s]"); %[output:0ffdc851]
ylabel("Required angle of attack, $\alpha$ [deg]"); %[output:0ffdc851]
title("Required Angle of Attack vs Airspeed"); %[output:0ffdc851]

%[text] ### Coordinated turn radius
figure("Name", "Turn Radius"); %[output:5f45900c]
hold on; %[output:5f45900c]
for i = 1:numel(turn)
    plot(V, turn(i).R, "LineWidth", 1.6, ... %[output:5f45900c]
        "DisplayName", sprintf("$%g^\\circ$ bank", turn(i).bank_deg)); %[output:5f45900c]
end
grid on; %[output:5f45900c]
xlabel("True airspeed, $V$ [m/s]"); %[output:5f45900c]
ylabel("Turn radius, $R$ [m]"); %[output:5f45900c]
title("Coordinated Level-Turn Radius"); %[output:5f45900c]
legend("Location", "best"); %[output:5f45900c]

%[text] ### Thrust and climb figures
%[text] These figures appear only after the propulsion model is populated.
if propulsionEnabled
    figure("Name", "Thrust Available and Required");
    plot(V, D_total, "LineWidth", 1.8, ...
        "DisplayName", "$T_{\mathrm{required}}=D$");
    hold on;
    plot(V, T_available, "LineWidth", 1.8, ...
        "DisplayName", "$T_{\mathrm{available}}$");
    grid on;
    xlabel("True airspeed, $V$ [m/s]");
    ylabel("Thrust [N]");
    title("Thrust Available and Required");
    legend("Location", "best");

    figure("Name", "Rate of Climb");
    plot(V, ROC, "LineWidth", 1.8);
    hold on;
    yline(0, ":");
    xline(V_bestROC, "--", "$V_y$", "Interpreter", "latex", "LabelVerticalAlignment", "bottom");
    grid on;
    xlabel("True airspeed, $V$ [m/s]");
    ylabel("Rate of climb, $\dot{h}$ [m/s]");
    title("Predicted Rate of Climb vs Airspeed");
end

%[text] ## 19. Next development steps
%[text] The next high-value additions are:
%[text] 1. Replace the placeholder propulsion block with a 12x6 propeller thrust map, $T(V,\\mathrm{RPM})$, or an equivalent motor-propeller model.
%[text] 2. Replace the reference mass with the measured ready-to-fly mass.
%[text] 3. Decide whether the paper's quoted $11\\,\\mathrm{m/s}$ stall speed is appropriate for the actual mass/configuration, or scale/re-measure the stall boundary.
%[text] 4. Add battery electrical power and propulsion efficiency so that aerodynamic power can be converted into current draw, range, and endurance.
%[text] 5. Add a separate power-off aerodynamic polar for glide/contingency analysis if required. \
%[text] 
%[text] These additions can be made without changing the fundamental aerodynamic derivations above.

